==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|scrupule}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|scrūpulus||uneasiness of mind, trouble, anxiety, doubt, scruple, literally a small sharp or pointed stone, the twenty-fourth part of an ounce}}, diminutive of {{m|la|scrūpus||a rough or sharp stone, anxiety, uneasiness}}; perhaps akin to {{etyl|grc|en}} {{m|grc|σκύρος||the chippings of stone}}, {{m|grc|ξυρόν||a razor}}, {{etyl|sa|en}} {{m|sa|क्षुर||a razor}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈskɹuː.pəl/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-us-scruple.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|uːpəl|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A weight of twenty [[grain]]s or one third of a [[dram]], abbreviated with the symbol [[℈]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Hence, a very small quantity; a particle.
#* Ca '''1601–1608''', [[w:William Shakespeare|Shakespeare]], ''As You Like It'', Act II Scene 3 221–222
#*: Paroles: ''I have not, my lord, deserved it.'' Lafeu: ''Yes, good faith, ev'ry dram of it, and I will not bate thee a '''scruple'''.''
# Hesitation as to action from the difficulty of determining what is right or expedient; unwillingness, doubt, or hesitation proceeding from motives of conscience.
#: ''He was made miserable by the conflict between his tastes and his '''scruples'''.'' - [[w:Thomas Babington Macaulay|Thomas Babington Macaulay]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A doubt or uncertainty concerning a matter of fact; intellectual perplexity.
# A [[Hebrew]] [[unit]] of [[time]], [[equal]] to {{frac|1080}} [[hour]].

